# findings.baseline — every row the design space currently reports, named once.
#
# THE GATE FIRES ON THE DIFFERENCE, not on the rows. A new row is red because
# nobody has looked at it; a vanished row is red because the baseline has
# stopped being true and a fix deserves a fixture. Same shape as
# tests/logos/bc_admits.ledger and logos_00_census_pin.
#
# Format:  <disposition> <question> <the question's own columns>
#
#   FALSE      28  each with a cause read out of the source or refuted with a
#                  one-variable control; gen_recv_struct_inner by a gdb
#                  breakpoint proving the kinds arrive and are answered elsewhere
#   NARROW     11  deliberate subsets, each with the reason stated at its site
#   OPEN       23  confirmed live or confirmed incomplete, all with repros and
#                  located mechanisms in docs/baghunt/
#   UNTRIAGED   0
#
# ⚠ THE DEBT IS PAID: 141 UNTRIAGED -> 0. The second half went to six agents,
# one per CLUSTER rather than per row — thirteen rows naming thirteen different
# missing callees turned out to be one defect, and splitting them would have
# been thirteen repetitions of one reading. Three groups closed as structural
# with causes read from the source; three produced confirmed live holes, all
# three adversarially verified.
#
# ⚠ AND ONE ROW I MARKED FALSE BY HAND WAS WRONG. I batch-marked the LetElse
# rows structural along with the rest of their group; the agent had said the
# group SPLITS and that those two carry a real admit whose root is in sema's
# divergence gate. Corrected to OPEN and filed. Marking on plausibility is the
# exact move this baseline exists to make impossible, and I made it anyway.
#
# ⚠ THE FIRST PAYDOWN WAS 164 -> 63 ROWS AND NOT ONE WAS DELETED. 71 duty rows
# went because the CLAIM was wrong and was corrected — it demanded the borrow
# checker's duties of gen_stmt, subst_stmt and seven other functions that owe
# nothing of the sort. 30 cluster rows went because `structural` could not
# locate system-header declarations and so failed to exclude `std::move` and
# `operator[]`. Both were the tool being wrong about itself, and both were
# pointed at by the tool's own cross-checks.
# ---- rows below
